  "related_work": "\\cite{KandirosPipisDaskalakisHarshaw2024CGD} supplies the fixed design, estimator, spectral variance bound, \\(o(n^{1/9})\\) sufficient CLT, and the unresolved inferential question; the proposal strictly strengthens those distributional and coverage results without changing their estimator algebra. \\cite{HeydeBrown1970DepartureNormality} and \\cite{Haeusler1988MartingaleRate} supply the martingale normal-approximation template, while \\cite{Bolthausen1982ExactMartingaleRates} and \\cite{Mourrat2013MartingaleRate} clarify why conditional-variance fluctuations can govern exact behavior. Theorem 2.3 of \\cite{JanischLehericy2024SparseDependencyGraph}, specialized to \\(\\delta=4\\), is the direct finite-fourth-moment dependency-graph substitution; Theorem 2.1 applies only to bounded summands, and Theorem 2.4 addresses \\(\\delta\\in(2,3)\\). These results, together with \\cite{Ross2011FundamentalsStein}, \\cite{Chin2019SteinInterference}, and \\cite{CaiShaoZhang2026RefinedLocalDependence}, inform the dependency-based comparison but are not claimed to be sharpened as general probability theorems. \\cite{KandirosHarshawSavje2026MinimaxNetwork} optimizes assignment designs and estimators for minimax mean-squared point-estimation risk under arbitrary neighborhood interference; it does not study the fixed \\(r=2\\) CGD estimator's Kolmogorov frontier or Wald coverage. \\cite{AronowSamii2017GeneralInterference} provides the exposure-weighted design-based inference baseline. \\cite{HarshawMiddletonSavje2026OptimizedVariance} establishes why inference should target an estimable conservative quadratic bound rather than the generally unidentified exact variance. Declaration 18.13 of \\cite{BlairCoppockHumphreys2023ResearchDesign}, modeled after \\cite{GreenKrasnoCoppockFarrerLenoirZingher2016LawnSigns}, supplies a concrete direct-effect consumer: its \\(238\\)-precinct queen-adjacency design defines \\(\\operatorname{mean}(Y_{1,0}-Y_{0,0})\\), exactly the en